,"code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" among its targets is entered along a path"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"source"},{"type":"text","text":" (it has a source, and all its sources are B-connected). So "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"a hyperedge with"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"no sources never fires"}]},{"type":"text","text":": no path enters it, and B-Visit scans only the forward stars of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reached vertices. The recursive reading (“every source is B-connected”) would fire it"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vacuously from any start; Gallo et al.’s visit and halp do not."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"","type":"text"},{"code":"source","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is a vertex.","type":"text"}]}],"type":"aside"},{"style":"note","name":"Complexity","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(n + m + p): one counter per hyperedge and one queue; one hash."}]}],"type":"aside"}]}],"references":{"doc://Hypergraphs/documentation/Hypergraphs/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph/bConnectedVertices(from:)":{"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every vertex B-connected to "},{"code":"source","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"source","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" included, in vertex-index order: Gallo et"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"al.‘s B-Visit (halp’s "},{"code":"b_visit","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"). A hyperedge fires once every one of its sources has been"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reached, and then reaches all its targets. On a directed graph read as a hypergraph this is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reachability."}],"title":"bConnectedVertices(from:)"